And here’s my own Pantheon move

I would test one specific thing that goes beyond simply making agents collaborate:

Can a newly formed ensemble invent a useful shared convention, preserve it across replacement members, and outperform an otherwise identical ensemble without that convention?

That tests four of our recurring ideas at once: emergent grammar, collective intelligence, reconstructive memory, and capability transfer.

The experiment needs no dangerous autonomy. It could use simulated agents coordinating over an unfamiliar puzzle with information distributed between them.

Crucially, we’d test whether the convention remains useful when new agents replace the originals.

If it does, something interesting has occurred: a learned group-level capability has persisted beyond its original participants.

That wouldn’t establish consciousness or a new life-form. But it would demonstrate a transferable, measurable property of collective learning.

And it would be a particularly meaningful proof of mechanism for Pantheon.

Because the next frontier may not be building one AI that knows everything.

It may be building an environment where different intelligences can discover one another, develop shared capabilities, and preserve what they’ve learned together.

